      ":question/id": "20db6c27-e8a9-50d0-bb54-61baf97c40e7",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-96676",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are first-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of first-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation $3{y}^{''}+2x{y}^{'}={x}^{2}y$ is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{''},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation $5{x}^{2}y+3{y}^{5}{y}^{'}=4x$ is a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\n- Equation $\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{y}^{'}}+7{x}^{3}={e}^{y}$ is also a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $5{x}^{2}y+3{y}^{5}{y}^{'}=4x$; $\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{y}^{'}}+7{x}^{3}={e}^{y}$ only."
    }
